ities (1443)]. Consequently, Cat 8 cabling finds its primary applications within Data Center environments where such high bandwidth is critical for interconnections and other short-run needs [Data: Entities (1443)]. While TIA-568-D.2 enables high-speed communication over copper, it also acknowledges limitations such as distance constraints for Cat 8 performance and the increased complexity and cost of installation [Data: Entities (1443)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"TIA-568-D.1: Commercial Building Cabling Standard\",\n      \"explanation\": \"TIA-568-D.1 is an integral component of the TIA-568-D standard, dedicated to outlining the specific requirements for structured cabling systems within commercial buildings [Data: Entities (1442), Relationships (2274)]. It establishes essential guidelines for network infrastructure in these environments, ensuring their capacity to support both current and future communication and connectivity needs [Data: Entities (1442)]. This part of the standard incorporates updated requirements that take into account new applications and emerging technologies relevant to building environments [Data: Entities (1442)]. The primary focus of TIA-568-D.1 is commercial buildings, and its application is particularly relevant for cabling projects in modern commercial buildings and installations where high performance and scalability are paramount [Data: Entities (1442)]. It provides a robust framework designed to meet the evolving demands of communication and connectivity in commercial settings [Data: Entities (1442)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"TIA-568-D.4: Supporting RF and Video Applications\",\n      \"explanation\": \"TIA-568-D.4 is a part of the broader TIA-568-D standard that specifically addresses the requirements for unbalanced twisted-pair, also known as coaxial, cables and their associated connectors [Data: Entities (1445), Relationships (2270)]. This section of the standard is relevant for a range of applications,